welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is Tina Liu professor of Chinese  literature at Yale professor Liu's  research interests include Chinese  fiction and drama during the period  between 1550 and 1750 today we'll talk  with her about her book accidental  incest filial cannibalism and other  peculiar encounters in late Imperial  Chinese literature welcome professor Liu  thank you for having me please tell me  about the premise of your book well I  started by reading through a lot of  texts in basically encountering a lot of  ways in which writers of the period  treated the idea of human community what  ties one individual to any other mm-hmm  okay and the title of your book is  fascinating how did you come to write  the book was there any particular  inspiration um you know the the period  of literature that I work on is full of  episodes that to a modern American seem  gross and so one of the things that the  book does is tries to situate these  gross moments so I you know I ended up  writing a book all about children  feeding their parents little bits of  their bodies and people running into  relatives when they go to brothels and  only afterwards that I come up with this  title which was something that a friend  and I cooked up at a coffee house okay  share some of the stories from your book  can you give us a couple of highlights  of perhaps the cannibalism or the incest  or something else that you found  particularly interesting well one of the  amazing things is that you know this  idea of filial love the love that we  have for our parents is something that  in lady Imperial China the the Naples  ultra of it was in fact  a kind of cannibalistic act there there  there parallels in medieval Europe where  there are records of women breastfeeding  their parents for example but in in the  Chinese case it's very very literal it's  called Google and basically you shave  off a bit of your arm or your leg and  prepare it in a stew for your parents  and why would you do that well the kind  of traditional medical response would be  that it was curative but one thing I try  to do in looking at a bunch of different  examples of this is to think about the  symbolic force of it which is  essentially and I think you see it  played out in a lot of different short  stories to where the the purpose is to  almost undo the usual generative force  of humanity which is that we give birth  to children and they give birth to  children and it's a kind of ever more  dilute relationship between us and our  descendants and this kind of cannibalism  almost tries to undo that spiraling  outwards and turns the relationship  between parent and child into something  that really can't go any further because  the parent is eating up the entire child  and in one story that I treat that's  literally what happens this  daughter-in-law sells herself at a  marketplace and instead of giving birth  to a much long four grandchild she is  consumed and the money that comes out of  selling her for meat saves the husband  but essentially the choice is made  between one kind of philly allottee and  the usual way in which families change  over time which is that they give rise  to new human beings all right that's a  fascinating story that a woman was so  sold for food for food yes I mean the  thing is it  in a lot of ways a lot of the stories  that I treat in the book are about  extreme situations you know kind of  famines that are so extreme that people  have to choose between living or eating  someone or selling themselves for meat  or dynastic crises in which people lose  track of their entire families I mean  you know I think I'm not even sure that  this kind of extreme circumstance really  ever took place or took place to any  great degree it's really a way to  meditate on what would happen if you  know I think it serves the same function  of science fiction in our culture where  it's not really as if we think that a  lot of these things can take place it's  more that we need to ask questions about  human identity about human relationships  and the way to do it is to find this  hypothetical which is a kind of extreme  circumstance okay that's interesting to  me so are you let's talk a little bit  about your methodology then so are you  saying that these stories that you've  you found somewhere are they true are  they not true or you don't know they're  fictional fiction okay  I work on fix you know some of them some  records of Google for example of the  filial cannibalism are real and they you  know I have every reason to believe that  they're they're factual there are  records of there are some interesting  literary type records of extreme Google  that again I have every reason to  believe are real but the things that I'm  most interested in are when that kind of  real experience is really refracted by  imagination okay so in let's talk about  again how you did the research how you  gathered up all of the stories did you  need to travel did you find things here  at Yale for instance what did you do  well I knew it Yale and most  this book was written before I came to  here also I have a lot of small children  so my research tends to be I sit around  and read books from the library that's  my fun actually okay so in terms of you  know that the time period that you're  interested in 1558 is 1750 what about  that time period intrigues you well I  mean I feel I mean I'm just an early  modern kind of person I love the way  that there's a kind of a gap between  popular culture and elite culture I but  that it's really interesting that there  are people who are active in both I  really love going to plays in my real  life and I love the fact that the  theater is so important in that age and  that people are interested in all sorts  of things that go hand in hand with  being interested in theater people are  interested in disguise and dress up and  they ask questions about gender that  seem really pertinent to me so you know  it's just to me the really really  interesting period of Chinese history  and in gathering up the stories and  assimilating them what are some of the  conclusions you reached in terms of the  human connections and community you  mentioned earlier well I mean when I  started researching the book what I was  hoping for was basically ways that  people at the time conceived of space  you know I was really interested in a  world where people don't actually travel  very much in that period and yet enough  people in a tiny minority do travel that  a lot of people are really interested in  the idea of what lies beyond their  immediate horizons so I thought there  would be all sorts of stuff about  spatial understanding and when I started  trying to impose that on the text I  really wasn't finding anything  particularly interest  down that road and what I realized was  it it's one way to one way to think  about space and the size of the human  community is the the the alternative  title for the book which I only came up  with after the book was published was  fancy meeting you here because that's  another way to think about how many  people there are in the world what are  the you know how many times have you sat  down on the train and you you saw  someone you sat down you go fancy  meeting you here what are the odds right  but that moment is really thematized in  Chinese literature of this period people  meditate on what it means when you run  into someone you know in and it is a way  of thinking about the population of what  it means to be a stranger what it means  to be a family member so you know that  that's the the how how the boat kind of  began okay and tell me what was one of  the most surprising things you found in  doing the research for the book I  shouldn't be surprised by this anymore  but what I found surprising was how  really deep and careful and methodical a  lot of these popular writers were  questions that we usually think of as  being anthropological I've often said  that traditional Chinese philosophy is  sufficiently narrow in its concerns you  know it's basically its ethics that a  lot of interesting intellectual work is  essentially pushed out into other forums  and so Chinese drama and fiction which  traditionally have been read as kind of  you know low forms really aren't you  know it's where a lot of people who  don't have any other way to ask certain  questions end up posing those questions  okay well thank you so much for being  here with us today and sharing some of  your work no thank you for having me for  more information about Professor Liu and  her work please visit our website at  Yale edu backslash Macmillan report be  sure to join us again for another  episode of the Macmillan report made  possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Center for  International and area studies at yeah  you
 

